The error occurs when Python tries to print an object. When Python tries to print an object, it internally calls the `__str__` method of the object. If this method is not defined, it then calls the `__repr__` method. If neither of these methods are defined, Python will default to a built-in basic printout of the object.

When either `__str__` or `__repr__` is called, Python attempts to access some default attributes of the object. If these attributes are not found, it eventually calls your `__getattr__` method, which prints "Sorry no key matches" when a key is not found in the dictionary.

To fix this issue, you can add `__str__` and/or `__repr__` methods to your class to define what should be printed when the object is printed. Here is an example:

```python
class JuspayObject:

    def __init__(self,response):
        self.__dict__['_response'] = response

    def __getattr__(self,key): 
        try:
            return self._response[key]
        except KeyError,err:
            sys.stderr.write('Sorry no key matches')

    def __str__(self):
        return str(self._response)

    def __repr__(self):
        return str(self)
```
In this code, when `print(test)` is called, it will now call the `__str__` method, which returns a string representation of the dictionary, so it won't raise a KeyError and won't call your `__getattr__` method in this case. If you want the output of `print(test)` to look different, you can modify the `__str__` method as you see fit. Similarly, you can modify the `__repr__` method to change the output when the object is printed in the console without a print statement.

