The `friend` keyword in C++ is used to grant access to private or protected members of a class to another class or function. It allows the friend class or function to access the private data of the class as if it were a member of the class. 

The main difference between declaring a function as `public` or `private` within the class and declaring it as a `friend` function is that public and private member functions are part of the interface of the class and are called by an object of the class, while a friend function is not a member of the class and is called independently.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```c++
class MyClass {
    private:
        int x;

    public:
        void publicFunc() { // part of the interface of the class
            x = 10;
        }

        friend void friendFunc(MyClass obj); // not part of the interface of the class
};

void friendFunc(MyClass obj) {
    obj.x = 20; // can access private member x of MyClass using the friend keyword
}

int main() {
    MyClass obj;
    obj.publicFunc(); // can access public member function of MyClass
    friendFunc(obj); // can access private member x of MyClass using the friend function
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, `publicFunc()` is part of the interface of the class `MyClass`, so it can be called by an object of the class. On the other hand, `friendFunc()` is not part of the interface of the class, but it can access the private member `x` of the `MyClass` object passed as an argument using the `friend` keyword.

