We observe a pattern that can simplify each term in the series. For any integer $n \geq 2$, we can write $\frac{n-1}{n!}$ as a difference of two fractions:
$$\frac{n-1}{n!} = \frac{1}{(n-1)!} - \frac{1}{n!}.$$

Using this identity, we rewrite the terms of the series:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2!} + \frac{2}{3!} + \frac{3}{4!} + \ldots + \frac{2015}{2016!}
&= \left(\frac{1}{1!} - \frac{1}{2!}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{2!} - \frac{1}{3!}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{3!} - \frac{1}{4!}\right) + \ldots + \left(\frac{1}{2015!} - \frac{1}{2016!}\right).
\end{align*}

When we sum the series, most terms cancel out due to the telescoping nature of the series:
\begin{align*}
\text{(Series)} &= \frac{1}{1!} - \frac{1}{2!} + \frac{1}{2!} - \frac{1}{3!} + \ldots + \frac{1}{2015!} - \frac{1}{2016!} \\
&= 1 - \frac{1}{2016!}.
\end{align*}

Therefore, the sum we are looking for is given by
$$ \boxed{1 - \frac{1}{2016!}}. $$

There are three methods to arrange the added three programs:
1. Arrange the three programs consecutively, which has $\binom{7}{1} \times 3!$ ways;
2. Arrange the three programs so that none of them are adjacent to each other, which has $P^3_7$ ways;
3. Exactly two of the programs are arranged consecutively, which has $\binom{3}{1} \times \binom{7}{1} \times \binom{6}{1} \times 2!$ ways.

According to the principle of counting by classification, the total number of arrangements is $\binom{7}{1} \times 3! + P^3_7 + \binom{3}{1} \times \binom{7}{1} \times \binom{6}{1} \times 2! = 504$ ways.

Therefore, the total number of arrangements is $\boxed{540}$ ways.

