By DeMoivre's Theorem,
\begin{align*}
\cos 5 \theta + i \sin 5 \theta &= (\cos \theta + i \sin \theta)^5 \\
&= \left(\frac{1}{4} + i \sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2}\right)^5 \\
&= \left(\frac{1}{4} + i \sqrt{\frac{15}{16}}\right)^5 \\
&= \left(\frac{1}{4} + i \frac{\sqrt{15}}{4}\right)^5.
\end{align*}
Expanding this using binomial theorem gives:
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{1}{4} + i \frac{\sqrt{15}}{4}\right)^5 &= \sum_{k=0}^{5} \binom{5}{k} \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^{5-k} \left(i \frac{\sqrt{15}}{4}\right)^k \\
&= \frac{1}{4^5} \left[1 - 5i \sqrt{15} - 50 \cdot \frac{15}{16} + 50i \frac{\sqrt{15}}{16} + 25 \cdot \frac{15^{3/2}}{64} + i \left(\frac{15^{5/2}}{256}\right) \right] \\
&= \frac{1}{1024} \left[1 - 5i \sqrt{15} - 750 + 50i \sqrt{15} + 125\sqrt{15} + i\sqrt{15^5} \right].
\end{align*}
Taking the real part gives:
\begin{align*}
\cos 5\theta &= \frac{1}{1024}\left(1 - 750 + 125\sqrt{15}\right) \\
&= \frac{1 - 750 + 125\sqrt{15}}{1024}.
\end{align*}
Which simplifies to:
\begin{align*}
\cos 5\theta &= \frac{125\sqrt{15} - 749}{1024} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{125\sqrt{15} - 749}{1024}}.
\end{align*}

